<!-- Website teaching layer for android/architecture/savedstatehandle.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it.
     Checked against developer.android.com (Saved State module for ViewModel; Parcelables and Bundles:
     1MB per-process Binder buffer, <50KB saved-state recommendation) on 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A user is halfway through a long support message when a two-factor code arrives. They switch to the authenticator app, then to a heavy game while they wait for a call back. Twenty minutes later they return: the support screen is open again, on the right ticket, but the message box is empty.</p>

The previous chapter's ViewModel did its job: it survived rotation. But this time the system killed the whole process, and the ViewModel went with it. Android already keeps a small saved-state **Bundle** for every activity the user can navigate back to, even after the process dies. `SavedStateHandle` connects that Bundle to your ViewModel, so a new ViewModel in a new process can start with the inputs the user had given. This chapter shows how the value travels, and why only small values belong there.

<!-- @after First Principles :: animation -->
### Follow the draft out of the process and back

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Saving and restoring through SavedStateHandle</span><div class="stage viz-stack"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">App process</span><div class="node" data-el="vm" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>SupportViewModel</code></strong><small data-text="vmNote">—</small></div><div class="node" data-el="handle" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>SavedStateHandle</code></strong><small data-text="handleNote">—</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">System process</span><div class="node" data-el="bundle" data-art="document" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Saved-state Bundle</strong><small data-text="bundleNote">—</small></div></div></div><div class="node is-key" data-el="event" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>What happens</strong><small data-text="eventNote">—</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Open the ticket","caption":"Navigation opens the screen with ticketId=812. The ViewModel's handle already contains that argument when the ViewModel is created.","add":["vm","handle","bundle","event"],"state":{"vm":"done","handle":"done","bundle":"ghost"},"text":{"vmNote":"instance #1","handleNote":"ticketId = 812","bundleNote":"nothing saved yet","eventNote":"argument from the navigation route"}},
{"label":"Type","caption":"Each edit updates the draft in the handle; getStateFlow(\"draft\", \"\") exposes that value. This shows the saving path, not a required text-input feedback loop.","state":{"handle":"active"},"text":{"handleNote":"ticketId = 812, draft = \"My order arrived…\"","eventNote":"savedStateHandle[\"draft\"] = text"}},
{"label":"Switch apps","caption":"The activity stops. Its SavedStateRegistry asks every registered provider, including this handle, for its values and writes them into the instance-state Bundle. The system process now holds that Bundle.","state":{"handle":"done","bundle":"done"},"text":{"bundleNote":"ticketId, draft (a few hundred bytes)","eventNote":"onStop() → onSaveInstanceState(): registry collects providers"}},
{"label":"Process killed","caption":"Memory is needed, so the system kills the process. The ViewModel and the handle object are gone. The Bundle is not: the system keeps it as long as the user can navigate back to this activity.","state":{"vm":"error","handle":"error","bundle":"done"},"text":{"vmNote":"gone","handleNote":"gone","eventNote":"no callback runs"}},
{"label":"Return from Recents","caption":"A new process recreates the activity and passes the Bundle back. The SavedStateRegistry restores it, and the ViewModel factory builds a new ViewModel with a handle that already contains ticketId and draft.","state":{"vm":"warn","handle":"done","bundle":"done"},"text":{"vmNote":"instance #2, new process","handleNote":"ticketId = 812, draft = \"My order arrived…\"","eventNote":"onCreate(bundle) → registry restored → factory creates the ViewModel"}},
{"label":"The screen rebuilds","caption":"getStateFlow emits the restored draft, so the text field shows it. The ticket itself is loaded again from the repository by its ID: only the ID and the user's input were saved.","state":{"vm":"done","handle":"active"},"text":{"vmNote":"loads ticket 812 from the repository","eventNote":"UI shows the draft; data reloads by ID"}}
]</script><figcaption>Only the user's input and the IDs travelled. Everything else was rebuilt from the data layer.</figcaption></figure>

For a Compose text editor, the IME-facing value must update synchronously. Do not debounce or asynchronously transform the value fed back to the field. A state-based `TextFieldState` can own editing while the app persists the draft; the [official migration guide](https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/compose/text/migrate-state-based) explains this boundary.

<!-- @diagram 19d28b22e8ce623d -->
### Two processes, one small parcel between them

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">App process: dies and restarts</span><div class="node"><strong>Activity / Fragment</strong><small>observes the ViewModel</small></div><div class="node"><strong>ViewModel</strong><small>reads and writes the handle</small></div><div class="node is-key"><strong><code>SavedStateHandle</code></strong><small>a provider in the owner's <code>SavedStateRegistry</code></small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">System process: survives</span><div class="node" data-state="done"><strong>Saved-state Bundle</strong><small>serialized when the activity stops; handed back when it is recreated</small></div></div></div><figcaption>The Bundle crosses the process boundary through Binder, which is why its size is limited.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @before Tradeoffs :: review -->
The size limit sentence in the next section ("1MB across the entire OS IPC system") is under technical review. A correction is pending the owner's approval.

<!-- @after Common Misconceptions :: check -->
**Handle, ViewModel field, or database?** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>The text of an unsent comment.</summary>

The handle is suitable for a small draft tied to this restorable screen. A draft that must survive deliberate task dismissal or be available on another device belongs in durable storage.
</details>

<details><summary>The 200 products shown on a search results screen.</summary>

Not the handle. Keep the query in the handle and the results in a cache or database, then reload by query after restoration.
</details>

<details><summary>A loading flag for a request in progress.</summary>

A ViewModel field. After process death the request is gone too, so restoring "loading = true" would show a spinner for work that is no longer running; derive it again when the new ViewModel starts.
</details>
